00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.697
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.697 --> 00:00:02.780
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to Google Cloud Drawing

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:05.540
Board, where we doodle
our way through the cloud.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:08.210
Today's topic-- what
is Compute Engine?

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:11.330
Simply put, it refers to
customizable virtual machines

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:13.400
running in Google Cloud.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.860
You can select the right
machine for your needs

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:18.320
from predefined or
custom machine types.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:21.260
Predefined machines come with
general-purpose combinations

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.070
of CPU and memory.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.930
But if those don't
meet your needs,

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:27.410
then you can choose the
custom machine types, which

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:30.500
allow the selection
of exact number of CPU

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.890
and amount of memory you
need for your workloads.

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:36.620
There are three different
machine-type families-- general

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:40.070
purpose, compute optimized,
and memory optimized.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:41.870
General purpose
machines are best

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:45.800
suited for general workloads,
like web servers and databases.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.290
If you're not sure which
family you should choose,

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:50.940
just choose general
purpose instances.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.960
It is recommended to use
compute optimized machines

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:55.910
for compute-intensive
applications,

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:58.730
such as high-performance
computing, gaming,

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:02.690
electronic design automation,
and single-threaded apps.

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:04.790
If you have
memory-intensive workloads,

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:09.710
such as in-memory databases, SAP
HANA, or real-time analytics,

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:12.125
use memory-optimized instances.

00:01:12.125 --> 00:01:14.570
And you can add graphical
processing units

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:16.610
to accelerate
computationally-intensive

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:20.690
workloads, like machine
learning or medical analysis.

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:23.360
Now, how do you
use Compute Engine?

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:24.740
Well, it's easy.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:28.040
Just select the type of
machine and where you want it,

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:30.470
and the instance gets
created for your use

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:32.720
in that specific location.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.730
There are some features
of Compute Engine

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:36.950
that make it an awesome choice.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:39.890
It allows for live
migration, so your app

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:42.200
can keep running
during maintenance mode

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.120
without interruptions.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.350
It provides sizing
recommendations.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:49.280
For example, if you are
using a bigger instance

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.010
for a workload that can
run on a smaller instance,

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:55.055
then you can save costs
using these recommendations.

00:01:55.055 --> 00:01:57.210
And for your
container workloads,

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:00.460
it supports deployment
of containers.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.830
Now, what does it cost
to use Compute Engine?

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:04.730
It is simple.

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:06.260
You pay for what you use.

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:10.220
But you can save costs by taking
advantage of some discounts.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:13.190
Sustained-use savings
are automatic discounts

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:16.370
applied for running instances
for a significant portion

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:17.540
of the month.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:19.700
If you know your
usage upfront, you

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.400
can take advantage of
committed-use discounts, which

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:27.510
can lead up to 57% savings
without any upfront cost.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:30.770
And by using short-lived,
preemptible instances,

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:33.380
you can save up to 80%.

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:37.610
These are great for batch jobs
and fault-tolerant workloads.

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:39.830
Along with lots of
other use cases,

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.950
like running websites and
databases on Compute Engine,

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.370
you can also use it to
migrate your existing systems

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:49.790
into Google Cloud, or even
run Windows applications

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:52.520
by bringing your own
licenses or using

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:55.150
the included licensed images.

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:57.010
To noodle more on
Compute Engine,

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:00.640
check out
cloud.google.com/compute.

